1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,260
So, the last thing in the agenda before we wrap up our end of the year meeting is choosing

2
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:10,600
the MVP of the year.

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:15,760
I know we don't typically give the most valuable person award to someone who didn't

4
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:21,280
work a complete year with us, but maybe we should make an exception.

5
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:25,560
Sarah Glassman has been phenomenal since she started with us.

6
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:29,200
Yes, she has done great.

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:37,200
Despite not having experience in sales, she helped us reach our goal of over 350 sales

8
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,720
in a month.

9
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:43,760
This has been something we've strived for since we opened eight years ago.

10
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:50,080
Yes, and by looking at this graph, it is clear she was a great hire.

11
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:54,080
Our sales have only continued to rise since she began.

12
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:58,440
I just wonder if the rest of the team will be disappointed.

13
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:04,280
They are longtime employees and may feel like she doesn't have the seniority that typically

14
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:06,840
comes with this reward.

15
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:13,640
Hmm, you may be right, but she gets along with everyone, and I believe everyone should

16
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:16,440
recognise her value and hard work.

17
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:20,160
If anything, it may inspire the rest of the team.

18
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:21,160
Good point.

19
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:23,760
OK, that is decided.

